// D-LIN1-DROP (ratified 2026-06-25): the deliberate-discard hatch for a
// `#SingleUse` value.
//
// A `#SingleUse` value carries a job that has to be done exactly once — the
// compiler proves it is consumed on every path (E0140/E0141/E0142). Almost
// always you want to *do* the job: release the lock, join the task. But once in
// a while you genuinely need to throw the value away without doing it — say the
// resource it guards is already gone. That is a real decision with consequences,
// so it has to be written down.
//
// The hatch is `consume(x)` inside an `#Unsafe("reason")` region or fn. The
// `#Unsafe` reason IS the audit note — there is no second audit channel. Outside
// an `#Unsafe` context, `drop` of a `#SingleUse` value is E0143, pointing you at
// the audited form.
//
// `drop` erases to a plain move-to-nowhere: the value's cleanup runs, no
// `unsafe` machine code is emitted. The `#Unsafe` gate is a compile-time audit,
// not a runtime cost.
#SingleUse struct Ticket {
    id: String
}

fn issue(id: String) -> Ticket {
    return Ticket.{id: ~id}
}

fn redeem(ticket: ^Ticket) {
    print("redeemed {ticket.id}")
}

fn run() {
    // The ordinary path: the ticket is consumed by being redeemed.
    a :: issue("A")
    redeem(^a)
    // The audited discard: this ticket is being voided on purpose — the event
    // it admitted to was cancelled, so there is nothing left to redeem. We say
    // so in the `#Unsafe` reason, and `drop` discharges the consume duty.
    b :: issue("B")
    #Unsafe("event cancelled; the ticket admits to nothing, so voiding it is correct") {
        consume(b)
    }
    print("ticket B voided")
}
